WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.382 --> 00:00:01.596
- As light goes into your eye,

00:00:01.596 --> 00:00:03.197
it first goes through your cornea,

00:00:03.197 --> 00:00:05.224
the protective outer surface of the eye.

00:00:05.224 --> 00:00:06.243
And then it hits your iris

00:00:06.243 --> 00:00:08.507
which is the colored part of your eye.

00:00:08.507 --> 00:00:11.598
The lens focuses the image
to the back of the retina

00:00:11.598 --> 00:00:13.135
and then the retina tissue is where

00:00:13.135 --> 00:00:16.141
the photoreceptor layers are
to detect the light impulses

00:00:16.141 --> 00:00:18.724
and send them through the
optic nerve back to the brain.

00:00:18.724 --> 00:00:20.974
(clicking)

00:00:27.592 --> 00:00:29.637
Our eyes are unique and different

00:00:29.637 --> 00:00:32.285
and everybody has a unique iris coloration

00:00:32.285 --> 00:00:34.340
like a fingerprint on their eye.

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:37.072
And so our perception is unique.

00:00:37.072 --> 00:00:40.989
Our eyes allow us to see
the good in the world.

00:00:41.896 --> 00:00:43.763
- Turns out that recognizing things

00:00:43.763 --> 00:00:46.010
is a really tough computational problem

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:48.815
and it costs a lot of neurons to do it.

00:00:48.815 --> 00:00:50.569
So, do we see goodness?

00:00:50.569 --> 00:00:51.711
When we're judging goodness

00:00:51.711 --> 00:00:54.528
I think typically that's
a beauty judgment.

00:00:54.528 --> 00:00:57.200
Spanish philosopher, George Santayana,

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:02.165
defined beauty as the pleasure
we attribute to the object.

00:01:02.165 --> 00:01:04.764
These individuals differences
and what we find beautiful

00:01:04.764 --> 00:01:08.530
are a very powerful and
common feature of our lives.

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:10.996
And this can apply in quite abstract ways.

00:01:10.996 --> 00:01:13.049
And it's not just literally
what the motion is

00:01:13.049 --> 00:01:14.948
but what it signifies.

00:01:14.948 --> 00:01:16.698
- Okay, come forward.

00:01:18.638 --> 00:01:20.512
Squint the eyes a little bit.

00:01:20.512 --> 00:01:21.345
Good.

00:01:22.352 --> 00:01:24.482
The most beautiful thing
that you see in a person

00:01:24.482 --> 00:01:26.775
is the beauty in their eyes.

00:01:26.775 --> 00:01:28.873
You can see their past, their presence

00:01:28.873 --> 00:01:30.452
and where they are right at this moment

00:01:30.452 --> 00:01:31.907
through the eyes.

00:01:31.907 --> 00:01:33.149
(clicking)

00:01:33.149 --> 00:01:35.441
So I did the first two
years of my modeling career

00:01:35.441 --> 00:01:37.146
you know, totally in a blur

00:01:37.146 --> 00:01:38.902
because I wasn't seeing well.

00:01:38.902 --> 00:01:41.339
And when I got my contacts and glasses

00:01:41.339 --> 00:01:43.557
I mean the whole world just opened up.

00:01:43.557 --> 00:01:45.058
It was just like amazing

00:01:45.058 --> 00:01:46.426
like this is the world.

00:01:46.426 --> 00:01:49.359
This is what I've been missing.

00:01:49.359 --> 00:01:51.932
- Eyes are critical in
order to see the world

00:01:51.932 --> 00:01:53.512
and perceive the good in the world.

00:01:53.512 --> 00:01:56.055
And so our perception is unique

00:01:56.055 --> 00:01:58.739
and allows us to be beautiful in our way

00:01:58.739 --> 00:02:00.494
as well as experience the beautiful colors

00:02:00.494 --> 00:02:02.680
in the world around us.

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:03.790
- When you see good deeds

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:06.645
you just have that compassion
feeling in your eyes

00:02:06.645 --> 00:02:09.346
and there's a warmth and a
feeling that overtakes you.

00:02:09.346 --> 00:02:10.263
It's great.

